\description{
The \code{compphysiol} package provides tools for building and simulating compartment
models in R. It separates model locations, modelled substances, processes,
auxiliary expressions, outputs, parameter values, and external inputs into
explicit model components.
}
\details{
Core modelling concepts include:
\itemize{
\item \code{compphysiol_model}: Encapsulates the structure of a compartmental model,
including compartments, molecules, transports, reactions, equations,
observables, parameters, and dosing events.
\item \code{compphysiol_compartments}: Represent model spaces, tissues, organs, or abstract
locations with names and volumes.
\item \code{compphysiol_molecules}: Represent modelled substances whose amounts or concentrations
can vary across compartments.
\item \code{compphysiol_transports}: Define movement of the same molecule between compartments.
\item \code{compphysiol_reactions}: Define molecular transformations or stoichiometric changes
within compartments.
\item \code{compphysiol_equations}: Define auxiliary expressions that can be reused in model
processes.
\item \code{compphysiol_observables}: Represent derived outputs calculated from states and
parameters.
\item \code{compphysiol_parameters}: Represent named inputs used in the definition of compartments,
transports, reactions, equations and observables.
\item \code{compphysiol_dosing}: Represents dosing events, including bolus and infusion doses.
}
